INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A media streaming service collects user interaction data, such as clicks, views, and search queries, in real time. This data is semi-structured and needs to be stored in a highly scalable, low-latency data store that can handle millions of requests per second with predictable performance. Analysts require flexible query capabilities for ad-hoc analysis and machine learning model training. The company wants to minimize the operational burden of managing the underlying infrastructure. Which combination of services should be used?', '["Amazon Redshift for real-time ingestion and analytical queries.","Amazon DynamoDB for storing interaction data, with Amazon S3 for archival and Amazon Redshift Spectrum for ad-hoc queries.","Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL for storing user interaction data and running analytical queries.","Amazon OpenSearch Service for real-time indexing and search, with Amazon S3 for long-term storage."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) provides a robust and scalable solution. Amazon DynamoDB is ideal for storing high-volume, low-latency semi-structured user interaction data due to its predictable performance at any scale and minimal operational overhead. For archival and flexible ad-hoc analysis, data can be exported to Amazon S3, which serves as a cost-effective data lake. Amazon Redshift Spectrum can then be used to query the data directly on Amazon S3 without loading it into Amazon Redshift, providing flexible analytical capabilities. Option A (incorrect) Amazon Redshift is an OLAP data warehouse optimized for analytical queries, not for high-volume, real-time ingestion of semi-structured data with millions of requests per second, and it would be an anti-pattern for this type of transactional or real-time operational data. Option C (incorrect) Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL is a relational database best suited for structured data and OLTP workloads, not for semi-structured data at the scale and real-time ingestion rates described, nor for flexible ad-hoc analysis on massive datasets. Option D (incorrect) Amazon OpenSearch Service is primarily for full-text search and log analytics. While it offers real-time indexing, it''s not the primary data store for raw interaction data for general analytical purposes and machine learning model training, and it would not be the most cost-effective or flexible solution for the full scope of requirements.', 33, 'm_33_1', 1, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A research institution collects petabytes of scientific data, including images, sensor readings, and experimental results, which are mostly unstructured or semi-structured. This data needs to be stored cost-effectively for long-term retention and made available for future analysis by various research teams. The access patterns are unpredictable, ranging from infrequent historical lookups to occasional large-scale batch processing. The institution wants to build a flexible, scalable repository for this diverse data. Which AWS service is the MOST suitable foundation for this requirement?', '["Amazon DynamoDB to store all data as documents for flexible access.","Amazon RDS for MySQL to store metadata and pointers to external files.","Amazon S3 as a data lake, integrated with AWS Lake Formation for governance.","Amazon Redshift for storing and querying all scientific data efficiently."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most suitable foundation. Amazon S3 is ideal for building a data lake, offering virtually unlimited, highly durable, and cost-effective storage for petabytes of unstructured and semi-structured data. Its flexibility allows for unpredictable access patterns and supports various analytical tools. Integrating with AWS Lake Formation provides centralized governance, security, and access control across the data lake. Option A (incorrect) Amazon DynamoDB is a NoSQL database optimized for key-value and document workloads with predictable performance at scale, but it is not cost-effective or designed for storing petabytes of unstructured data like images and large sensor readings. Option B (incorrect) Amazon RDS for MySQL is a relational database and is not suitable for storing petabytes of unstructured or semi-structured data directly. Storing only metadata and pointers would add complexity and still require another service for the actual data storage. Option D (incorrect) Amazon Redshift is an OLAP data warehouse optimized for structured data and analytical queries. It is not designed for cost-effectively storing petabytes of raw, unstructured, or semi-structured data, and it would be prohibitively expensive for this use case.', 33, 'm_33_1', 2, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A global e-commerce company manages diverse datasets: customer profiles (key-value), product catalogs (relational), and real-time clickstream events (time-series). Each dataset has distinct access patterns, scalability requirements, and data structures. The company wants to implement a data architecture that optimizes performance and cost for each data type while minimizing the effort to integrate and manage these disparate stores. Which architectural principle should guide the selection of data stores?', '["Consolidate all data into a single Amazon Redshift cluster for unified analytics.","Store all data in a single Amazon S3 data lake and use AWS Glue for schema inference.","Implement polyglot persistence, using Amazon DynamoDB, Amazon RDS, and Amazon Timestream.","Utilize Amazon OpenSearch Service for all data, leveraging its search capabilities for diverse access patterns."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) aligns with the principle of polyglot persistence, which is the most effective approach for this scenario. It involves choosing the best data store for each specific data type and access pattern. Amazon DynamoDB is excellent for key-value customer profiles, Amazon RDS for relational product catalogs, and Amazon Timestream for real-time time-series clickstream events. This optimizes performance and cost while minimizing management effort for each specific workload. Option A (incorrect) is an anti-pattern (ap-2.1-002). Consolidating all data into a single Amazon Redshift cluster would be inefficient and costly for key-value and time-series data, as Redshift is optimized for OLAP on structured data. Option B (incorrect) while a data lake on Amazon S3 is a good foundation for raw data, it''s not a direct ''data store'' for real-time operational access patterns like key-value or time-series. It would require additional services and layers to meet the performance and integration requirements, and it doesn''t directly address the ''optimize performance and cost for each data type'' for operational workloads. Option D (incorrect) Amazon OpenSearch Service is specialized for full-text search and log analytics. While powerful for certain access patterns, it is not designed to be a primary data store for all types of data, such as relational product catalogs or raw time-series data, and would be suboptimal for many of the described workloads.', 33, 'm_33_1', 3, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A research team regularly performs complex analytical queries on large datasets stored in an Amazon S3 data lake. The data is in various formats, including Parquet and CSV. The team needs to join this data with smaller, highly structured reference tables that are frequently updated and stored in an Amazon Redshift cluster. They want to perform these joins and analyses without moving the large S3 datasets into Redshift, to minimize data loading overhead and storage costs. Which AWS service allows this federated query capability?', '["Amazon Redshift Spectrum.","AWS Glue Data Catalog with Amazon Athena.","Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL with dblink extension.","Amazon DynamoDB with S3 export and import functionality."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the ideal solution. Amazon Redshift Spectrum allows Amazon Redshift to query data directly on Amazon S3, enabling federated queries that join data in S3 with data in Redshift tables. This eliminates the need to load large datasets into Redshift, saving storage costs and reducing data loading overhead, directly meeting the stated requirements. Option B (incorrect) AWS Glue Data Catalog with Amazon Athena can query data directly on S3, but it cannot directly join data with tables residing within an Amazon Redshift cluster in a single query. It would require moving data or separate query steps. Option C (incorrect) Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL with dblink is for querying other PostgreSQL databases or compatible relational databases, not for querying data directly on Amazon S3. Option D (incorrect) Amazon DynamoDB with S3 export and import functionality is for moving data between DynamoDB and S3, not for performing federated queries between S3 and Redshift in real-time for analytical purposes.', 33, 'm_33_1', 4, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A global logistics company processes high-volume, real-time sensor data from thousands of delivery vehicles. Each data point consists of a vehicle ID, timestamp, and location, requiring immediate lookup by vehicle ID for tracking applications. The data must also be available for analytical queries later. The company needs a data store that offers the **LOWEST latency for individual record lookups** by vehicle ID.', '["Implement Amazon DynamoDB with the vehicle ID as the partition key.","Store data in Amazon S3 as individual objects with vehicle ID as part of the key.","Utilize Amazon Redshift to ingest and query the sensor data.","Deploy Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL and store data in a relational table."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most suitable choice. Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed, serverless NoSQL database service designed for high-performance, low-latency key-value and document workloads, making it ideal for immediate lookups by vehicle ID. Its ability to scale to massive request volumes with consistent single-digit millisecond latency directly addresses the requirement for lowest latency for individual record lookups.
Option B (incorrect) would involve storing data in Amazon S3. While S3 is highly scalable and cost-effective for data lakes, it is object storage and not optimized for low-latency, individual record lookups. Retrieving individual small objects from S3 would introduce higher latency and operational complexity compared to a purpose-built key-value store. This approach embodies the anti-pattern [ap-2.1-002] of not evaluating polyglot persistence for different data characteristics.
Option C (incorrect) suggests Amazon Redshift. Amazon Redshift is an OLAP data warehouse optimized for large-scale analytical queries over vast datasets, not for low-latency transactional or key-value lookups. Using it for individual record lookups would be inefficient and costly, violating the primary constraint. This represents the anti-pattern [ap-2.1-001] of using Amazon Redshift for transactional OLTP workloads.
Option D (incorrect) proposes Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL. While RDS can handle transactional workloads, for extremely high-volume, low-latency key-value lookups, DynamoDB offers superior performance, scalability, and lower operational overhead. RDS would require more management and might not achieve the same consistent low latency at scale for this specific access pattern, embodying the anti-pattern [ap-2.1-002] by not choosing the optimal data store for key-value access patterns.', 33, 'm_33_1', 5, NULL, NULL);

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_33_4
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A data engineering team is designing a new data warehouse using Amazon Redshift to store several terabytes of sales data. The data will be queried frequently by sales analysts, often filtering by `sale_date` and `region_id`. To optimize query performance and minimize storage costs, the team needs to choose an effective schema design strategy. Which approach should they prioritize for the `sales` table?', '["Use a single distribution key for `sale_id` and no sort key.","Define `sale_date` as a distribution key and `region_id` as a sort key.","Implement a `DISTSTYLE ALL` distribution for the `sales` table.","Store all data as JSON documents in a single column without a defined schema."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is an effective schema design strategy for Amazon Redshift. Defining `sale_date` as a distribution key will distribute data across compute nodes based on the date, which is good if queries frequently filter or join on this column. Defining `region_id` as a sort key will physically order the data within each slice by region, significantly improving performance for queries that filter or range-scan on `region_id` and `sale_date`. Option A (incorrect) using a single distribution key for `sale_id` (a unique identifier) would likely lead to an even distribution but wouldn''t optimize for common analytical filters like `sale_date` or `region_id`, and lacking a sort key would hinder query performance. Option C (incorrect) implementing `DISTSTYLE ALL` for a large fact table like `sales` (terabytes of data) would replicate the entire table to all compute nodes. This would drastically increase storage costs and data loading times, and is only suitable for small dimension tables. Option D (incorrect) storing all data as JSON documents in a single column without a defined schema is an anti-pattern for Amazon Redshift. Redshift is a columnar database optimized for structured data and analytical queries, and this approach would severely degrade query performance and complicate data analysis.', 33, 'm_33_4', 6, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A mobile gaming company uses Amazon DynamoDB to store player profiles, including user IDs, game progress, and in-game purchases. The primary access pattern is retrieving a player''s profile by `user_id`. However, the marketing team frequently needs to query players based on `last_login_date` to target inactive users or by `total_purchases` to identify high-value customers. To support these secondary access patterns efficiently, what should the data engineering team implement?', '["Perform full table scans on the DynamoDB table for each marketing query.","Create a Global Secondary Index (GSI) on `last_login_date` and another GSI on `total_purchases`.","Use Amazon Redshift to store a copy of the DynamoDB data for all analytical queries.","Add `last_login_date` and `total_purchases` as attributes to the primary key."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most efficient way to support secondary access patterns in Amazon DynamoDB. Global Secondary Indexes (GSIs) allow you to query the table using an alternate key, separate from the primary key, providing flexibility for different query patterns. Creating GSIs on `last_login_date` and `total_purchases` will enable efficient retrieval of players based on these attributes without impacting the performance of primary key lookups. Option A (incorrect) performing full table scans for marketing queries would be extremely inefficient, costly, and would not scale with the growing number of players, leading to unacceptable latency. Option C (incorrect) while using Amazon Redshift for analytical queries is a valid pattern, it introduces data replication, potential latency for data freshness, and additional operational overhead compared to leveraging DynamoDB''s native indexing capabilities for these specific query patterns. Option D (incorrect) adding `last_login_date` and `total_purchases` as attributes to the primary key (assuming `user_id` is the partition key) would not enable efficient querying by these attributes independently. It would only allow querying if these attributes were part of a composite sort key, and even then, it would not support querying by `last_login_date` or `total_purchases` as the primary filter efficiently without the `user_id`.', 33, 'm_33_4', 7, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A healthcare provider stores patient records, medical images, and lab results in an Amazon S3 data lake. The schema for lab results frequently evolves as new tests are introduced or existing test formats change. Analysts need to query historical and current data consistently, even with schema variations. The data engineering team must ensure that schema changes do not break existing queries and that new data can be seamlessly integrated. Which approach should they adopt for schema evolution?', '["Require all historical data to be re-processed and converted to the latest schema version.","Store all data in a single, rigid schema in Amazon Redshift, rejecting any data that does not conform.","Implement a schema-on-read approach using AWS Glue Data Catalog, allowing different schema versions to coexist.","Manually update all data processing jobs and analytical queries every time a schema change occurs."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the recommended approach for schema evolution in a data lake. A schema-on-read approach, facilitated by AWS Glue Data Catalog, allows different schema versions of the same dataset to coexist. When data is queried, the appropriate schema version is applied, ensuring that historical queries continue to work and new data with evolved schemas can be integrated seamlessly. This provides flexibility and resilience to schema changes. Option A (incorrect) re-processing and converting all historical data to the latest schema version is a time-consuming, resource-intensive, and often impractical task for large datasets, especially with frequent schema changes. Option B (incorrect) storing all data in a single, rigid schema in Amazon Redshift is suitable for a data warehouse with stable schemas but is an anti-pattern for evolving semi-structured data in a data lake, as it would lead to data rejection and inflexibility. Option D (incorrect) manually updating all data processing jobs and analytical queries for every schema change is highly inefficient, error-prone, and unsustainable, especially in environments with frequent schema evolution.', 33, 'm_33_4', 8, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A financial institution is building a data lake on Amazon S3 to store transaction data. This data arrives daily in various formats (CSV, JSON, Parquet) and new fields are occasionally added to the source systems. The data engineering team needs to ensure that analysts can query the most current data using Amazon Athena, even with schema changes, while minimizing manual effort for schema updates. Which approach meets these requirements with the **LEAST operational overhead**?', '["Manually update the schema definitions in the AWS Glue Data Catalog whenever a new field is introduced.","Configure AWS Glue Crawlers to automatically infer and update schemas in the AWS Glue Data Catalog on a schedule.","Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to directly query the S3 data, relying on external table definitions that are manually maintained.","Implement a custom Lambda function to parse schema changes from source systems and update the AWS Glue Data Catalog."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most effective solution. Configuring AWS Glue Crawlers to run on a schedule allows them to automatically infer schemas from new data in Amazon S3 and update the corresponding table definitions in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. This approach handles schema evolution with minimal manual intervention, ensuring that Amazon Athena users always have access to the most current schema without requiring engineers to manually track and apply changes, thus providing the least operational overhead.
Option A (incorrect) involves manually updating schema definitions. This approach is prone to errors, time-consuming, and does not scale with frequent schema changes, leading to significant operational overhead and potential delays in data availability for analysts. This directly contradicts the requirement for minimal manual effort.
Option C (incorrect) suggests using Amazon Redshift Spectrum with manually maintained external table definitions. While Redshift Spectrum can query S3 data, manually maintaining table definitions for schema evolution is cumbersome and increases operational overhead, similar to Option A. Furthermore, Redshift Spectrum is primarily for analytical queries from Redshift, not the primary mechanism for schema management for a data lake.
Option D (incorrect) proposes implementing a custom AWS Lambda function. While technically feasible, developing, testing, and maintaining a custom Lambda function to parse schema changes and update the AWS Glue Data Catalog introduces significant development and operational overhead compared to the out-of-the-box capabilities of AWS Glue Crawlers, which are designed for this exact purpose.', 33, 'm_33_4', 9, NULL, NULL);

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_33_3
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A data analytics company uses Amazon Redshift to store several terabytes of customer behavior data. The data is loaded daily, and analysts frequently query this data to generate reports. The company observes that query performance has degraded over time, and storage utilization is higher than expected. Upon investigation, they find that deleted rows are still occupying disk space, and data is not optimally organized. Which Amazon Redshift maintenance operation should the data engineering team regularly perform to address these issues and optimize performance and storage?', '["Execute `INSERT` statements to re-insert all data into new tables.","Perform a `VACUUM` operation to reclaim space and sort rows, followed by an `ANALYZE` operation.","Migrate all data to Amazon S3 and use Amazon Redshift Spectrum for all queries.","Increase the cluster size by adding more compute nodes to improve query performance."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the appropriate Amazon Redshift maintenance operation. The `VACUUM` command reclaims space from deleted rows and re-sorts data according to the sort keys, which significantly improves query performance. The `ANALYZE` command updates table statistics, allowing the query optimizer to generate more efficient query plans. Regularly performing both `VACUUM` and `ANALYZE` is crucial for maintaining optimal Redshift performance and storage efficiency. Option A (incorrect) executing `INSERT` statements to re-insert all data into new tables is a manual, inefficient, and disruptive way to achieve what `VACUUM` does automatically. Option C (incorrect) migrating all data to Amazon S3 and using Amazon Redshift Spectrum for all queries would change the fundamental architecture and might not be suitable for all types of analytical workloads that benefit from Redshift''s performance, and it doesn''t address the underlying maintenance needs of the existing Redshift cluster. Option D (incorrect) increasing the cluster size might temporarily mask performance issues but does not address the root cause of un-reclaimed space or unsorted data, leading to higher costs without optimal efficiency.', 33, 'm_33_3', 10, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A manufacturing company stores sensor data from IoT devices in an Amazon S3 data lake. This data is initially accessed frequently for real-time dashboards for the first 30 days, then infrequently for another 60 days for compliance auditing, and finally needs to be archived for 7 years for regulatory compliance, with very rare access. The company wants to minimize storage costs while ensuring data availability according to access patterns. Which S3 Lifecycle policy configuration is MOST cost-effective?', '["Transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive immediately after creation.","Keep all objects in S3 Standard storage class indefinitely.","Transition objects to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days, then to S3 Glacier after 90 days (30+60).","Transition objects to S3 Intelligent-Tiering after 30 days, then to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 90 days."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most cost-effective S3 Lifecycle policy. It aligns perfectly with the described access patterns: S3 Standard for the first 30 days (frequent access), S3 Standard-IA for the next 60 days (infrequent access for auditing), and then S3 Glacier for long-term archival (7 years, very rare access). This strategy optimizes costs by moving data to cheaper storage classes as its access frequency decreases. Option A (incorrect) transitioning objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive immediately after creation would make the data unavailable for real-time dashboards and frequent auditing for the initial periods, violating the access requirements. Option B (incorrect) keeping all objects in S3 Standard storage class indefinitely is an anti-pattern (ap-2.3-001) as it is the most expensive tier and would significantly increase storage costs for data that becomes infrequently accessed or archived. Option D (incorrect) S3 Intelligent-Tiering is good for unknown or changing access patterns, but for clearly defined access patterns like this, manually configuring transitions to Standard-IA and Glacier is often more cost-effective. Additionally, transitioning to Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 90 days is a valid archival step, but the initial transition to Intelligent-Tiering might incur slightly higher costs than direct Standard-IA for known infrequent access.', 33, 'm_33_3', 11, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A media company stores large video files in an Amazon S3 bucket. These files are frequently accessed for the first 90 days after upload, then become rarely accessed but must be retained for legal compliance for 5 years. After 5 years, they can be deleted. The company wants to implement a lifecycle policy that automatically moves these files to lower-cost storage classes and eventually deletes them, without manual intervention, to optimize storage costs. Which S3 Lifecycle policy configuration meets these requirements?', '["Keep all objects in S3 Standard storage class for 5 years, then delete them.","Transition objects to S3 Standard-IA after 90 days, then expire objects after 5 years.","Transition objects to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 90 days, then expire objects after 5 years.","Transition objects to S3 Intelligent-Tiering after 90 days, with no expiration."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most cost-effective and compliant S3 Lifecycle policy. After 90 days of frequent access (in S3 Standard), transitioning to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval is appropriate for rarely accessed data that needs to be retained for 5 years. S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval offers significantly lower storage costs for archival data. Finally, expiring the objects after 5 years ensures compliance with the deletion requirement. Option A (incorrect) keeping all objects in S3 Standard storage class for 5 years is an anti-pattern (ap-2.3-001) as it is the most expensive tier and would result in unnecessarily high storage costs for rarely accessed data. Option B (incorrect) transitioning to S3 Standard-IA is suitable for infrequently accessed data, but for data that is ''rarely accessed'' and needs to be archived for 5 years, S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval or S3 Glacier Deep Archive would be more cost-effective. Option D (incorrect) S3 Intelligent-Tiering is useful for unknown or changing access patterns, but for clearly defined access patterns like this, direct transitions are often more cost-effective. Also, having ''no expiration'' would violate the requirement to delete files after 5 years.', 33, 'm_33_3', 12, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A data analytics platform stores customer event logs in an Amazon S3 bucket. These logs are generated continuously, and the platform needs to retain every version of an object for auditing purposes. Additionally, the logs are typically accessed for analysis within the first 30 days, become infrequently accessed for the next 60 days, and then need to be archived for 10 years for compliance. The company wants to minimize storage costs while maintaining all versions and meeting access patterns. Which S3 configuration and lifecycle policy should be implemented?', '["Enable S3 Object Lock and keep all objects in S3 Standard for 10 years.","Enable S3 Versioning, then transition all object versions to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 30 days.","Enable S3 Versioning, transition current versions to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days, then to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 90 days, and expire noncurrent versions after 10 years.","Enable S3 Intelligent-Tiering for all objects and disable S3 Versioning to save costs."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) provides the most comprehensive and cost-effective solution. Enabling S3 Versioning ensures that every version of an object is retained for auditing. The lifecycle policy transitions current versions to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days (for infrequent access), then to S3 Glacier Flexible Retrieval after 90 days (for archival). Expiring noncurrent versions after 10 years ensures compliance with the long-term retention requirement while managing storage costs for older versions. Option A (incorrect) S3 Object Lock provides WORM compliance but does not manage different access patterns or versions for cost optimization. Keeping all objects in S3 Standard for 10 years is an anti-pattern (ap-2.3-001) and would be extremely expensive. Option B (incorrect) transitioning all object versions to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 30 days would make the data unavailable for frequent analysis within the first 30 days and for infrequent access in the next 60 days, violating the access requirements. Option D (incorrect) enabling S3 Intelligent-Tiering is good for unknown access patterns, but disabling S3 Versioning would violate the requirement to retain every version for auditing. For known access patterns, explicit lifecycle rules are often more cost-effective.', 33, 'm_33_3', 13, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A data science team is working with a large dataset of customer reviews stored in an Amazon S3 bucket. The access patterns for this data are highly unpredictable; sometimes, the data is accessed frequently for new model training, and other times, it remains untouched for months. The team wants to optimize storage costs without constantly monitoring and adjusting storage classes. They also need to ensure that the data is readily available when needed. Which S3 storage class is BEST suited for this scenario?', '["Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering.","Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA).","Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive.","Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA)."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the best choice for unpredictable access patterns. Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering automatically moves objects between two access tiers (frequently accessed and infrequently accessed) based on changing access patterns. This helps optimize storage costs without performance impact or operational overhead, as the data is always readily available. Option B (incorrect) Amazon S3 Standard-IA is designed for data that is accessed less frequently but requires rapid access when needed. It''s suitable when access patterns are known to be infrequent, but not for highly unpredictable patterns where data might become frequently accessed again. Option C (incorrect) Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive is for long-term archival of data that is accessed very rarely (once or twice a year), with retrieval times in hours. This would not meet the requirement for data to be ''readily available when needed'' for unpredictable access. Option D (incorrect) Amazon S3 One Zone-IA is similar to S3 Standard-IA but stores data in a single Availability Zone, offering lower cost but less resilience. It''s not suitable for unpredictable access patterns and has lower durability.', 33, 'm_33_3', 14, NULL, NULL);

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_33_2
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A company has a large Amazon S3 data lake containing various datasets, including customer transactions, website clickstreams, and IoT sensor data. Data analysts and business users across different departments need to discover, understand, and query this data. However, without proper metadata, they struggle to find relevant datasets, understand their schemas, or trust the data''s quality. This has led to a ''data swamp'' situation. Which AWS service is essential to address this problem and enable data discovery and governance?', '["Amazon Redshift for centralized data storage and querying.","Amazon DynamoDB for storing metadata about the S3 objects.","AWS Glue Data Catalog as a centralized metadata repository.","Amazon OpenSearch Service for full-text search capabilities over S3 objects."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is essential for addressing the ''data swamp'' problem. AWS Glue Data Catalog serves as a centralized metadata repository for all data assets in a data lake. It stores schema definitions, table locations, and other metadata, making data discoverable and understandable for analysts and business users. This prevents the data lake from becoming a ''data swamp'' (ap-2.2-001) by providing structure and context to raw data. Option A (incorrect) Amazon Redshift is an OLAP data warehouse and is not designed to be a metadata repository for an S3 data lake. It would require data to be loaded into it, which is not the goal of cataloging existing S3 data. Option B (incorrect) while Amazon DynamoDB could technically store metadata, it would require significant custom development for schema inference, versioning, and integration with other analytical tools, making it a less efficient and more operationally intensive solution compared to AWS Glue Data Catalog. Option D (incorrect) Amazon OpenSearch Service provides full-text search capabilities, but it is not a data catalog. It would require pre-indexing and would not inherently provide schema information, data lineage, or governance features needed for a comprehensive data catalog.', 33, 'm_33_2', 15, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A large enterprise is building a new data lake on Amazon S3. They need to ensure that data access is strictly controlled, with different departments having varying levels of permissions to specific datasets. Additionally, they want to simplify the process of granting and revoking access, and enforce row-level and column-level security. Which AWS service provides a centralized approach to manage permissions and secure the data lake?', '["AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) policies directly on S3 buckets.","AWS Lake Formation for centralized data lake governance and access control.","Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL to store access control lists for S3 objects.","AWS Key Management Service (KMS) for encrypting data and controlling access to keys."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most suitable service. AWS Lake Formation provides a centralized and simplified way to manage data lake governance and access control. It allows administrators to define granular permissions (table-level, column-level, and row-level) on data stored in Amazon S3 and registered in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. It integrates with various analytics services, making it easy to grant and revoke access for different users and groups. Option A (incorrect) while AWS IAM policies can control access to S3 buckets, managing granular permissions (especially row-level and column-level) directly with IAM policies on S3 buckets becomes complex and cumbersome for a large data lake with diverse user groups and varying data access needs. Option C (incorrect) Amazon RDS for PostgreSQL is a relational database and is not designed to be an access control system for an S3 data lake. Implementing custom access control logic in RDS would introduce significant operational overhead and complexity. Option D (incorrect) AWS Key Management Service (KMS) is used for managing encryption keys and encrypting data. While crucial for data security, it does not provide the granular data access control and governance capabilities for data lakes that AWS Lake Formation offers.', 33, 'm_33_2', 16, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A retail company stores daily sales reports, customer feedback, and inventory updates in an Amazon S3 data lake. These files are typically in CSV or JSON format. The data engineering team needs to automatically discover the schema of these files, register them in a central metadata repository, and track schema changes over time to ensure data quality and discoverability for analysts. Which AWS Glue feature should they use to automate this process?', '["AWS Glue Data Catalog directly for manual schema definition.","AWS Glue Crawlers to automatically infer schemas and populate the Data Catalog.","AWS Glue DataBrew for data profiling and schema validation.","AWS Glue Studio for building visual data integration workflows."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the appropriate AWS Glue feature. AWS Glue Crawlers automatically connect to data stores (like Amazon S3), infer the schema and data types of the data (CSV, JSON, Parquet, etc.), and then populate or update the metadata in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. This automates the process of schema discovery and tracking schema changes, making data discoverable and usable for analysts. Option A (incorrect) while AWS Glue Data Catalog is the central metadata repository, manually defining schemas for a large and evolving data lake is time-consuming and error-prone, defeating the purpose of automation. Option C (incorrect) AWS Glue DataBrew is a visual data preparation tool used for data profiling, cleaning, and normalization, but it does not automatically infer and register schemas in the Data Catalog. Option D (incorrect) AWS Glue Studio is a visual interface for creating, running, and monitoring AWS Glue extract, transform, and load (ETL) jobs, but it''s not designed for automatic schema inference and cataloging.', 33, 'm_33_2', 17, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A startup is rapidly accumulating various datasets, including application logs, user activity, and third-party data feeds, all stored in an Amazon S3 data lake. The data is growing quickly, and different teams are starting to use it for diverse analytical purposes. However, without a clear understanding of what data exists, its structure, or its quality, teams are struggling to find and utilize relevant information, leading to duplicated efforts and distrust in data. What is the primary consequence of not implementing a data catalog in this scenario?', '["Increased Amazon S3 storage costs due to unmanaged data growth.","Reduced data security because access controls cannot be applied effectively.","The data lake will become a ''data swamp,'' hindering data discovery and usability.","Slower data ingestion rates into the Amazon S3 data lake."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) describes the primary consequence of not implementing a data catalog, which is an anti-pattern (ap-2.2-001). Without a data catalog (like AWS Glue Data Catalog), data lakes become ''data swamps'' where data assets are undiscoverable, their schemas are unknown, and their quality and lineage are untracked. This severely hinders data discovery, usability, and trust for analysts and business users. Option A (incorrect) while unmanaged data growth can increase S3 costs, the lack of a catalog primarily impacts data usability and discovery, not directly the storage cost. Lifecycle policies manage cost more directly. Option B (incorrect) data security is managed primarily through services like AWS IAM and AWS Lake Formation. While a catalog aids in applying granular controls, its absence doesn''t directly mean access controls cannot be applied at all, but rather that it''s harder to manage and understand what to secure. Option D (incorrect) data ingestion rates into S3 are typically independent of whether a data catalog is in place. The cataloging process usually happens after data lands in S3.', 33, 'm_33_2', 18, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A large enterprise with multiple business units wants to implement a data mesh architecture. Each business unit owns and manages its data products independently, but these products need to be discoverable, shareable, and governed centrally across the entire organization. The enterprise requires a solution that enables data producers to publish their data products and data consumers to easily find, understand, and subscribe to them, while maintaining a unified data governance framework. Which AWS service is designed for these data mesh and data portal use cases?', '["AWS Glue Data Catalog for schema management and metadata storage.","Amazon Redshift for centralized analytical processing and data sharing.","Amazon DataZone for data mesh, data portal, and data product management.","AWS Lake Formation for data lake security and access control."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is specifically designed for data mesh and data portal use cases. Amazon DataZone provides a data catalog, data governance, and a data portal that allows data producers to publish data products and data consumers to discover, use, and subscribe to them. It facilitates a federated approach to data management across an enterprise, aligning with the principles of a data mesh architecture. Option A (incorrect) AWS Glue Data Catalog is a foundational component for metadata management in a data lake, but it does not provide the full suite of features for data product management, data portals, or the collaborative aspects of a data mesh that Amazon DataZone offers. Option B (incorrect) Amazon Redshift is an analytical data warehouse. While it can be part of a data mesh, it is not the service that enables the overarching data mesh architecture, data product discovery, or governance framework. Option D (incorrect) AWS Lake Formation is primarily focused on data lake security, access control, and simplifying permissions management for data in S3 and the Glue Data Catalog. While important for governance, it does not provide the data product publishing, discovery, and subscription capabilities required for a data mesh portal.', 33, 'm_33_2', 19, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775005113845', 'A large enterprise has a centralized Amazon S3 data lake receiving data from various departments. Data analysts from different teams need to discover available datasets, understand their schemas, and access them with fine-grained permissions. The enterprise requires a solution that ensures **data discoverability and controlled access** across the data lake with a unified metadata layer.', '["Rely solely on Amazon S3 bucket policies and object ACLs for access control and documentation in external wikis for discoverability.","Implement AWS Glue Data Catalog for metadata storage and integrate it with AWS Lake Formation for centralized governance and access control.","Use Amazon DataZone to manage data assets, schemas, and access permissions for the data lake.","Develop a custom metadata service using Amazon RDS to store schema information and manage user access."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) provides the most robust and integrated solution for data discoverability and controlled access in an Amazon S3 data lake. AWS Glue Data Catalog serves as a centralized metadata repository, making datasets and their schemas discoverable. Integrating it with AWS Lake Formation allows for fine-grained access control, security, and auditing across the data lake, ensuring that analysts can only access the data they are authorized for, all through a unified governance layer.
Option A (incorrect) relies on Amazon S3 bucket policies and ACLs, which offer coarse-grained access control at the bucket or object level but lack the fine-grained, table-level, or column-level permissions required for complex enterprise data lakes. Documenting data in external wikis does not provide a centralized, programmatic, or query-engine-integrated discoverability mechanism, leading to a ''data swamp'' scenario. This embodies the anti-pattern [ap-2.2-001] of not maintaining a data catalog for a data lake, allowing a ''data swamp''.
Option C (incorrect) suggests using Amazon DataZone. Amazon DataZone is designed for data mesh architectures and creating data portals for data producers and consumers to share and discover data products. While it can integrate with AWS Glue Data Catalog and Lake Formation, it is a higher-level service for data collaboration and governance at an organizational scale, rather than the core underlying metadata and access control layer for the data lake itself. It''s not the primary service for establishing the unified metadata layer and fine-grained access control at the data lake level.
Option D (incorrect) proposes developing a custom metadata service using Amazon RDS. This approach would introduce significant operational overhead for development, maintenance, and integration with data lake query engines (like Amazon Athena or Amazon Redshift Spectrum). It would also lack the native integration and features for data lake governance that AWS Glue Data Catalog and AWS Lake Formation provide.', 33, 'm_33_2', 20, NULL, NULL);

