INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A data engineering team is migrating an on-premises data warehouse to Amazon Redshift. The source database contains several large tables with complex data types and stored procedures. The team needs to efficiently convert the existing schema and data to Amazon Redshift''s columnar format, ensuring data type compatibility and optimizing for analytical queries with LEAST manual effort.', '["Option A: Manually rewrite all DDL statements and stored procedures to be compatible with Amazon Redshift syntax and data types.","Option B: Use the AWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT) to automate the conversion of schema, data types, and code objects to Amazon Redshift.","Option C: Export data to CSV files, manually create new tables in Amazon Redshift, and then import the data using the COPY command.","Option D: Utilize AWS Glue crawlers to infer the schema from the source database and then apply it directly to Amazon Redshift tables."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most efficient approach. The AWS Schema Conversion Tool (AWS SCT) is specifically designed to automate the conversion of existing database schemas and code objects (like stored procedures, functions, and views) from a wide range of source databases to Amazon Redshift, minimizing manual effort and potential errors. Option A (incorrect) would be extremely time-consuming, error-prone, and require significant manual effort, directly contradicting the ''LEAST manual effort'' constraint. Option C (incorrect) addresses data migration but not schema conversion, and manually creating tables for complex schemas would still be a significant manual effort. Option D (incorrect) is used for inferring schemas from data sources like Amazon S3 or JDBC connections for the AWS Glue Data Catalog, but it is not designed for converting complex database schemas and stored procedures to Amazon Redshift''s specific syntax and data types.', 33, 'm_33_4', 6, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A data lake stores customer interaction data in Amazon S3, with schemas defined in AWS Glue Data Catalog. The data arrives daily in Parquet format, and new fields are occasionally added to the source system. Data scientists query this data using Amazon Athena. The team needs to manage schema evolution to ensure that new data is queryable without breaking existing queries, while minimizing data transformation overhead.', '["Option A: Force all new data to conform to the original schema by rejecting records with new fields, ensuring strict schema enforcement.","Option B: Manually update the AWS Glue Data Catalog table schema every time a new field is introduced in the source data.","Option C: Implement a schema evolution strategy that allows new fields to be added as nullable columns, preserving backward compatibility for existing queries.","Option D: Re-ingest all historical data with the new schema every time a change occurs, overwriting previous versions to maintain a single schema."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most effective strategy for schema evolution in a data lake. Allowing new fields to be added as nullable columns in the schema (e.g., in Parquet files and reflected in AWS Glue Data Catalog) ensures backward compatibility. Existing queries that do not reference the new fields will continue to work without modification, and new queries can access the updated schema. Option A (incorrect) would lead to data loss or incomplete data, as it discards valuable new information, which is generally undesirable in a data lake. Option B (incorrect) would be a high-overhead manual process, especially with frequent schema changes, and could still lead to query failures if not managed carefully. Option D (incorrect) would be extremely inefficient and costly for large datasets, as it involves re-processing and re-storing petabytes of data for every minor schema change, significantly increasing operational overhead and storage costs.', 33, 'm_33_4', 7, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A data engineer is designing a new Amazon Redshift cluster to store sales transaction data. The data includes a timestamp column and is frequently queried to analyze sales trends over specific periods. To optimize query performance and reduce storage costs for large tables, the engineer needs to implement an effective data modeling strategy.', '["Option A: Store all data in a single, large table without any distribution or sort keys, relying on Redshift''s default behavior.","Option B: Use a single distribution key on a non-unique column and no sort keys, to simplify data loading.","Option C: Implement a distribution key based on a frequently joined column and a compound sort key on the timestamp and another frequently filtered column.","Option D: Partition the table manually into many smaller tables based on the sales date, managing each partition separately."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) represents an optimal data modeling strategy for Amazon Redshift. Using a distribution key on a frequently joined column ensures that related data is co-located on the same compute nodes, minimizing data movement during joins. A compound sort key on the timestamp (for range-restricted queries) and another frequently filtered column significantly improves query performance by allowing Redshift to quickly skip large portions of data that do not match the filter criteria. Option A (incorrect) would lead to poor query performance and inefficient storage utilization, as Redshift would have to scan more data and perform more data movement. Option B (incorrect) would not provide optimal performance for analytical queries, especially those involving joins and time-based filtering, as it doesn''t leverage Redshift''s query optimization features effectively. Option D (incorrect) is an anti-pattern for Redshift; Redshift''s internal partitioning (sort keys) and distribution keys are designed to handle large tables efficiently, and manual partitioning into many smaller tables would increase management complexity and potentially degrade performance for cross-partition queries.', 33, 'm_33_4', 8, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A data lake stores immutable historical sensor data in Amazon S3, organized by date. Data scientists frequently query this data using Amazon Athena, often filtering by specific date ranges. To improve query performance and reduce the amount of data scanned, the data engineer needs to optimize the data organization and schema definition.', '["Option A: Store all sensor data in a single S3 bucket without any folder structure, relying on Athena''s full table scan capabilities.","Option B: Implement S3 object versioning for all sensor data to track changes, and define a single, non-partitioned table in AWS Glue Data Catalog.","Option C: Organize the data in S3 using a Hive-style partitioning scheme (e.g., `s3://bucket/sensor_data/year=YYYY/month=MM/day=DD/`) and define a partitioned table in AWS Glue Data Catalog.","Option D: Compress all sensor data using a general-purpose compression algorithm and store it in a single, large JSON file per day."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most effective way to optimize query performance and reduce data scanned for time-series data in a data lake. Organizing data with a Hive-style partitioning scheme in Amazon S3 (e.g., `year=YYYY/month=MM/day=DD/`) allows Amazon Athena to leverage partition pruning. When queries filter by date, Athena only scans the relevant S3 prefixes (partitions), significantly reducing the amount of data processed and thus improving performance and lowering costs. Option A (incorrect) would force Athena to scan the entire dataset for every query, leading to high costs and slow performance. Option B (incorrect) is useful for data recovery and auditing but does not improve query performance or reduce data scanned for analytical queries, and a non-partitioned table would still lead to full scans. Option D (incorrect) might reduce storage size but storing all data in a single large JSON file per day would still require Athena to read the entire file for filtering, losing the benefits of columnar formats like Parquet and efficient partitioning.', 33, 'm_33_4', 9, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A company is ingesting real-time streaming data into an Amazon S3 data lake. The schema of the incoming data is dynamic, with new fields occasionally appearing. Data analysts need to query this data using Amazon Athena. The data engineer must design a solution that automatically detects schema changes and updates the AWS Glue Data Catalog to reflect these changes, ensuring data discoverability and query compatibility.', '["Option A: Manually inspect incoming data for schema changes and update the AWS Glue Data Catalog table definitions accordingly.","Option B: Use AWS Glue crawlers scheduled to run periodically on the Amazon S3 data to automatically detect schema changes and update the AWS Glue Data Catalog.","Option C: Enforce a strict schema on the incoming streaming data, rejecting any records that do not conform to the predefined schema.","Option D: Store all incoming data as raw JSON files in Amazon S3 and rely on Amazon Athena''s schema-on-read capabilities without using AWS Glue Data Catalog."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most efficient and automated way to handle schema evolution in a data lake. AWS Glue crawlers can be scheduled to run periodically over the Amazon S3 data. They automatically infer schema changes, including new fields, and update the corresponding table definitions in the AWS Glue Data Catalog, ensuring that data analysts can query the latest schema with Amazon Athena without manual intervention. Option A (incorrect) would be a high-overhead, error-prone manual process, especially with dynamic schemas and large data volumes. Option C (incorrect) would lead to data loss for records with new, valid fields, which is generally undesirable for a data lake where all raw data is typically preserved. Option D (incorrect) would mean that analysts would have to manually define schemas for every query, which is cumbersome and defeats the purpose of a centralized data catalog for discoverability and consistent schema management.', 33, 'm_33_4', 10, NULL, NULL);

