INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A media company stores large volumes of video archives in Amazon S3. These videos are rarely accessed after 30 days but must be retained for legal compliance for 10 years. The company needs to minimize storage costs while ensuring data is retrievable within a few hours when needed. The solution should be automated and require LEAST operational overhead.', '["Option A: Store all video archives in Amazon S3 Standard storage class for 10 years and manually move them to Glacier if needed.","Option B: Implement a custom AWS Lambda function to monitor object age and move objects to Amazon S3 Glacier after 30 days.","Option C: Configure an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days, then to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 90 days, with a 10-year expiration.","Option D: Store all video archives in Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive immediately upon upload to achieve the lowest possible storage cost."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most cost-effective and operationally efficient solution. An S3 Lifecycle policy automates the transition of objects between storage classes based on predefined rules. Transitioning to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days for infrequent access, then to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 90 days for long-term archival (with retrieval in hours), significantly reduces storage costs while meeting the 10-year retention requirement and minimizing operational overhead. Option A (incorrect) is an anti-pattern (ap-2.3-001) because storing all data in S3 Standard for 10 years is very expensive for rarely accessed data and requires manual intervention for cost optimization. Option B (incorrect) would introduce unnecessary operational overhead for developing, deploying, and maintaining a custom Lambda function, which is less efficient than using native S3 Lifecycle policies. Option D (incorrect) would achieve the lowest cost but might not meet the ''retrievable within a few hours'' requirement, as S3 Glacier Deep Archive retrievals can take up to 12 hours, and it''s not ideal for data that might be accessed within the first 90 days, even if infrequently.', 33, 'm_33_3', 11, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A healthcare organization stores patient records in Amazon S3. Due to strict regulatory requirements, these records must be immutable and retained for a minimum of 7 years, preventing any accidental or malicious deletion or modification during this period. The solution must provide Write Once, Read Many (WORM) protection.', '["Option A: Enable S3 Versioning on the bucket to keep multiple versions of objects, ensuring data can be restored.","Option B: Implement S3 Object Lock in Compliance mode with a retention period of 7 years on the S3 bucket.","Option C: Configure an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects to S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 7 years, and delete them.","Option D: Use AWS Identity and Access Management (IAM) policies to restrict delete permissions on the S3 bucket for all users."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the appropriate solution for WORM compliance. S3 Object Lock, when configured in Compliance mode with a specified retention period (e.g., 7 years), prevents an object from being overwritten or deleted by any user, including the root account, until the retention period expires. This meets the strict immutability and WORM requirements. Option A (incorrect) helps protect against accidental deletion or overwrites by keeping previous versions, but it does not prevent the deletion of all versions or the bucket itself, and it doesn''t enforce a strict WORM compliance period. Option C (incorrect) is for cost optimization and data retention but does not provide WORM protection against modification or deletion during the retention period; it only manages transitions and eventual deletion. Option D (incorrect) provides access control but can be circumvented by users with sufficient permissions (e.g., root user or an administrator who can modify IAM policies), and it does not provide the immutable WORM guarantee that Object Lock offers.', 33, 'm_33_3', 12, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A data analytics team uploads daily batches of customer interaction logs to an Amazon S3 bucket. Access patterns for these logs are unpredictable: some logs might be accessed frequently for a few days, then infrequently, and eventually become cold archival data after a few months. The company wants to optimize storage costs automatically without manual intervention or complex lifecycle rule management.', '["Option A: Configure the S3 bucket to use S3 Intelligent-Tiering storage class.","Option B: Apply an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects from S3 Standard to S3 Standard-IA after 30 days, then to S3 Glacier after 90 days.","Option C: Store all log data in S3 Standard storage class indefinitely.","Option D: Use S3 Glacier Deep Archive for all incoming log data to achieve the lowest possible storage cost from day one."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most suitable solution for unpredictable access patterns. Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering automatically moves objects between two access tiers (frequent access and infrequent access) based on changing access patterns, optimizing storage costs without performance impact or retrieval fees. It also includes an optional archive access tier for rarely accessed data. This eliminates the need for manual lifecycle rule management. Option B (incorrect) requires predefined rules, which might not be optimal for unpredictable access patterns and could incur unnecessary retrieval fees if data is accessed frequently after being moved to Standard-IA or Glacier. Option C (incorrect) is an anti-pattern (ap-2.3-001) because storing all data in S3 Standard indefinitely is the most expensive option and does not optimize costs for infrequently accessed data. Option D (incorrect) would result in high retrieval costs and long retrieval times, making it unsuitable for data that might be accessed frequently in the initial days or weeks.', 33, 'm_33_3', 13, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A company needs to archive large volumes of sensor data that are rarely accessed but must be retained for regulatory compliance for 20 years. The primary goal is to achieve the ABSOLUTE LOWEST storage cost, and retrieval times of up to 12 hours are acceptable. Data is uploaded once and never modified.', '["Option A: Store data in Amazon S3 Standard-Infrequent Access (S3 Standard-IA) for 20 years.","Option B: Configure an S3 Lifecycle policy to transition objects to Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive after 0 days.","Option C: Store data in Amazon S3 Intelligent-Tiering to automatically optimize costs.","Option D: Use Amazon S3 One Zone-Infrequent Access (S3 One Zone-IA) for all archival data."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most cost-effective solution for long-term archival with acceptable retrieval times of up to 12 hours. Amazon S3 Glacier Deep Archive offers the lowest storage cost among all S3 storage classes, making it ideal for data that is rarely accessed and needs to be retained for many years. Transitioning immediately (after 0 days) ensures the lowest cost from the start. Option A (incorrect) is more expensive than Glacier Deep Archive and is designed for data that is still accessed infrequently but requires faster retrieval (milliseconds). Option C (incorrect) is designed for unpredictable access patterns and is more expensive than Glacier Deep Archive for purely archival data. Option D (incorrect) is also more expensive than Glacier Deep Archive and is designed for infrequently accessed data that can tolerate being stored in a single Availability Zone, which might not be suitable for critical archival data that needs maximum durability.', 33, 'm_33_3', 14, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A data pipeline generates temporary processing files in an Amazon S3 bucket daily. These files are only needed for 7 days for debugging purposes and then must be permanently deleted to minimize storage costs and comply with data retention policies. The process should be fully automated.', '["Option A: Manually delete files older than 7 days from the S3 bucket using the AWS Management Console or AWS CLI.","Option B: Implement a custom AWS Lambda function triggered daily to identify and delete old files in the S3 bucket.","Option C: Configure an S3 Lifecycle policy to expire objects after 7 days.","Option D: Enable S3 Versioning on the bucket and manually delete old versions of files after 7 days."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most efficient and automated solution. An S3 Lifecycle policy can be configured to expire objects after a specified number of days (e.g., 7 days), automatically and permanently deleting them from the bucket. This minimizes storage costs and requires no operational overhead. Option A (incorrect) is a manual process that is prone to human error and does not scale, failing the ''fully automated'' requirement. Option B (incorrect) introduces unnecessary operational overhead for developing, deploying, and maintaining a custom Lambda function, which is less efficient than using native S3 Lifecycle policies. Option D (incorrect) would keep multiple versions of files, increasing storage costs, and still requires manual intervention to delete old versions, failing the ''fully automated'' and ''minimize storage costs'' requirements.', 33, 'm_33_3', 15, NULL, NULL);

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_33_2
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A company stores various datasets in an Amazon S3 data lake, including raw logs, processed data, and analytics results. Data scientists and analysts need to easily discover available datasets, understand their schemas, and access them using query engines like Amazon Athena. The company wants to prevent a ''data swamp'' and enable centralized metadata management.', '["Option A: Rely on data scientists to manually document dataset schemas and locations in internal wikis.","Option B: Implement AWS Lake Formation to centralize metadata management, security, and access control for the data lake.","Option C: Store all data in Amazon Redshift, using its internal catalog for schema management.","Option D: Use AWS Glue Data Catalog as a centralized metadata repository for all S3 datasets, populating it with AWS Glue crawlers."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{3}'::integer[], 'Option D (correct) is the most direct and effective solution for centralized metadata management and preventing a ''data swamp''. AWS Glue Data Catalog acts as a persistent, centralized metadata repository for all data assets, including those in Amazon S3. AWS Glue crawlers can automatically infer schemas and populate the catalog, making datasets discoverable and queryable by services like Amazon Athena. Option A (incorrect) is an anti-pattern (ap-2.2-001) as it leads to a ''data swamp'' due to lack of discoverability, inconsistent documentation, and high manual overhead. Option B (incorrect) is a comprehensive solution for data lake governance and access control, but the core component for centralized metadata is the AWS Glue Data Catalog, which Lake Formation uses. While Lake Formation builds upon Glue Data Catalog, the most direct answer for ''centralized metadata repository'' and ''preventing a data swamp'' is Glue Data Catalog itself, with crawlers for population. Option C (incorrect) is for OLAP data warehousing, not for managing metadata of diverse datasets across an S3 data lake.', 33, 'm_33_2', 16, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A data engineering team is building a new data lake on Amazon S3. They need to ensure that data consumers (analysts, data scientists) can easily discover datasets, understand their structure, and access them securely. The primary goal is to establish a centralized metadata repository and fine-grained access control for specific tables and columns within the data lake.', '["Option A: Use AWS Lake Formation to manage data lake permissions and integrate with AWS Glue Data Catalog for metadata.","Option B: Implement a custom application to manage user access and metadata for S3 objects.","Option C: Grant direct Amazon S3 bucket policies to users for read access, and rely on file naming conventions for discovery.","Option D: Store all data in Amazon DynamoDB and use its IAM integration for fine-grained access control."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most comprehensive solution. AWS Lake Formation simplifies building, securing, and managing data lakes by integrating with AWS Glue Data Catalog for metadata management and providing a centralized console for fine-grained access control down to the table and column level. This ensures data discoverability and secure access. Option B (incorrect) would introduce significant operational overhead and complexity for developing and maintaining a custom access control and metadata management system, which is less secure and scalable than AWS-managed services. Option C (incorrect) is an anti-pattern (ap-2.2-001) as it lacks centralized metadata, making data discovery difficult (''data swamp''), and S3 bucket policies are coarse-grained, making fine-grained access control challenging. Option D (incorrect) is a NoSQL database and not suitable for building a data lake on Amazon S3, nor does it provide a centralized catalog for S3 data.', 33, 'm_33_2', 17, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A data engineer has ingested new datasets into an Amazon S3 data lake. These datasets are organized in a Hive-style partitioning scheme. To make these new partitions discoverable and queryable by Amazon Athena, the engineer needs to update the AWS Glue Data Catalog. The goal is to automate the process of adding new partitions to existing tables.', '["Option A: Manually add each new partition to the AWS Glue Data Catalog using the AWS Management Console or AWS CLI.","Option B: Run an AWS Glue crawler periodically over the S3 path to automatically discover and add new partitions to the existing table in the AWS Glue Data Catalog.","Option C: Use Amazon Redshift Spectrum to directly query the S3 data without needing to update the AWS Glue Data Catalog.","Option D: Create a new AWS Glue table for each new partition, linking them together in a custom script for querying."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most efficient and automated method. AWS Glue crawlers can be configured to run on a schedule or on-demand over an S3 path. They automatically detect new partitions added to a dataset and update the corresponding table definition in the AWS Glue Data Catalog, making them immediately available for querying by Amazon Athena and other services. Option A (incorrect) is a manual, time-consuming, and error-prone process, especially for frequently updated datasets, failing the automation requirement. Option C (incorrect) still relies on the AWS Glue Data Catalog for schema and partition information; Redshift Spectrum queries external data defined in Glue Data Catalog, so updating the catalog is still necessary. Option D (incorrect) would create an unmanageable number of tables and significantly increase complexity, making data discovery and querying extremely difficult.', 33, 'm_33_2', 18, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A company''s data lake on Amazon S3 receives daily updates to several datasets, with new data files added to existing partitions. Data analysts use Amazon Athena to query this data. The data engineer needs to ensure that the AWS Glue Data Catalog is always up-to-date with the latest data files and partitions, providing accurate metadata for Athena queries, with LEAST operational overhead.', '["Option A: Manually run `ALTER TABLE ADD PARTITION` commands in Athena for every new partition.","Option B: Develop and maintain a custom AWS Lambda function to detect new S3 files and update Glue Data Catalog partitions via API calls.","Option C: Schedule AWS Glue crawlers to run daily on the relevant S3 paths to discover new data and update the AWS Glue Data Catalog.","Option D: Rely on Amazon Athena''s `MSCK REPAIR TABLE` command to automatically discover new partitions, bypassing AWS Glue crawlers."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most effective and low-overhead solution. Scheduling AWS Glue crawlers to run periodically (e.g., daily) on the Amazon S3 paths where new data is added allows them to automatically discover new files and partitions, and then update the corresponding table definitions in the AWS Glue Data Catalog. This ensures the catalog is always up-to-date for Amazon Athena queries with minimal operational effort. Option A (incorrect) is a manual process that is not scalable and introduces significant operational overhead. Option B (incorrect) involves developing and maintaining custom code, which increases operational overhead compared to using a fully managed service like AWS Glue crawlers. Option D (incorrect) is a valid command for Athena, but it requires the user to run it manually or integrate it into a separate orchestration, and it might not discover all schema changes as comprehensively as a Glue crawler, which is designed for this purpose.', 33, 'm_33_2', 19, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A data analytics team is struggling to find and understand the various datasets stored in their Amazon S3 data lake. Different departments upload data in various formats without consistent naming conventions or schema definitions. This has led to a ''data swamp'' where data is difficult to use. The team needs to implement a solution to improve data discoverability and governance.', '["Option A: Implement a strict naming convention for all S3 objects and require manual documentation for each dataset.","Option B: Use Amazon Redshift as the central repository for all data, enforcing a unified schema for all incoming datasets.","Option C: Deploy Amazon OpenSearch Service to index all S3 object names and allow full-text search for data discovery.","Option D: Utilize AWS Glue Data Catalog, populated by AWS Glue crawlers, to centralize metadata and enable data discovery and schema definition for S3 datasets."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{3}'::integer[], 'Option D (correct) directly addresses the problem of a ''data swamp'' and improves data discoverability and governance. AWS Glue Data Catalog provides a centralized metadata repository for all data assets in the data lake, including those in Amazon S3. AWS Glue crawlers automatically infer schemas and populate the catalog, making datasets discoverable and queryable by services like Amazon Athena. Option A (incorrect) is an anti-pattern (ap-2.2-001) as it relies on manual processes, which are prone to inconsistencies and do not scale, ultimately failing to prevent a ''data swamp''. Option B (incorrect) is an anti-pattern (ap-2.1-002) because Amazon Redshift is an OLAP data warehouse, not a data lake, and forcing all diverse datasets into a single relational schema is often impractical and inefficient for a data lake''s purpose. Option C (incorrect) is designed for full-text search on indexed data, but it would only index object names, not infer schemas or provide comprehensive metadata for data governance and discoverability within a data lake context.', 33, 'm_33_2', 20, NULL, NULL);

