INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A data analytics company stores petabytes of raw, uncompressed CSV data in an Amazon S3 bucket. Analysts frequently run ad-hoc SQL queries against this data using Amazon Athena. The company is concerned about high query costs and slow performance. They need to optimize their data lake for the MOST cost-effective querying with Amazon Athena.', '["Convert the CSV data to Apache Parquet format and partition it by relevant columns in Amazon S3.","Increase the Amazon Athena query engine memory settings to process the uncompressed CSV files faster.","Migrate the data from Amazon S3 to an Amazon Redshift cluster and use Redshift Spectrum for querying.","Implement a custom AWS Lambda function to pre-filter the CSV data before Amazon Athena queries run."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most cost-effective solution. Converting data to a columnar format like Apache Parquet significantly reduces the amount of data scanned by Amazon Athena, as Athena charges per byte scanned. Partitioning further narrows down the data scanned for specific queries, drastically reducing costs and improving performance. This directly addresses the anti-pattern of querying uncompressed, non-columnar S3 data with Athena (ap-3.2-001).
Option B (incorrect) is not a valid Athena optimization. Athena is serverless, and users do not control engine memory settings directly. This would not address the fundamental issue of scanning large amounts of uncompressed data.
Option C (incorrect) would introduce significant operational overhead and cost. Amazon Redshift is a managed data warehouse, not a serverless query service for S3 data. While Redshift Spectrum can query S3, maintaining a Redshift cluster for ad-hoc queries on raw S3 data would be more expensive and complex than optimizing Athena.
Option D (incorrect) would add complexity and operational overhead. While pre-filtering might reduce data scanned, it requires developing and maintaining a custom Lambda function, which is less efficient and more complex than leveraging Athena''s native capabilities with optimized data formats and partitioning.', 34, 'm_34_2', 1, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A marketing department needs to visualize customer engagement data stored in Amazon S3. The data is already processed and available in a structured format. The team requires interactive dashboards and reports with minimal setup and ongoing maintenance. Which solution provides the LEAST operational overhead for creating these visualizations?', '["Develop custom dashboards using a self-managed BI tool on Amazon EC2 instances connected to Amazon S3.","Use Amazon QuickSight to create interactive dashboards directly from the data in Amazon S3.","Set up an Amazon EMR Studio cluster and use Apache Zeppelin notebooks for data visualization.","Load the data into an Amazon Redshift cluster and connect a third-party BI tool for reporting."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) provides the least operational overhead. Amazon QuickSight is a fully managed, serverless business intelligence service that can directly connect to data in Amazon S3. It requires no server provisioning, patching, or maintenance, making it ideal for teams focused on data analysis rather than infrastructure management.
Option A (incorrect) involves significant operational overhead. Managing EC2 instances, installing a BI tool, and maintaining its infrastructure would be a heavy burden for the marketing department.
Option C (incorrect) would introduce more operational overhead than QuickSight. Amazon EMR Studio is excellent for interactive data exploration and development, but setting up and managing an EMR cluster, even with EMR Studio, is more complex and resource-intensive than using a fully managed BI service for dashboards.
Option D (incorrect) would also increase operational overhead and cost. Managing an Amazon Redshift cluster and integrating a third-party BI tool requires database administration, performance tuning, and connector management, which is more complex than QuickSight''s serverless approach.', 34, 'm_34_2', 2, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A team of data scientists needs an environment for interactive data exploration, experimentation, and notebook-based development using Apache Spark. They work with large datasets stored in an Amazon S3 data lake and require a collaborative platform that simplifies cluster management. Which AWS service BEST meets these requirements for interactive data exploration?', '["Use Amazon Athena for interactive SQL queries on the Amazon S3 data.","Deploy a self-managed Apache Spark cluster on Amazon EC2 instances.","Utilize Amazon EMR Studio for collaborative, notebook-based data exploration with Apache Spark.","Load the data into Amazon Redshift and use its query editor for interactive analysis."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the best solution for interactive, notebook-based data exploration with Apache Spark. Amazon EMR Studio provides a fully managed, collaborative development environment that integrates with Amazon EMR clusters, allowing data scientists to easily run Spark notebooks, explore data, and share their work without managing underlying infrastructure.
Option A (incorrect) is suitable for interactive SQL queries but does not support notebook-based development or Apache Spark for more complex data transformations and machine learning tasks.
Option B (incorrect) would involve significant operational overhead. Managing a self-managed Apache Spark cluster on EC2 instances requires manual provisioning, configuration, scaling, and maintenance, which is precisely what EMR Studio aims to simplify.
Option D (incorrect) is designed for data warehousing and complex analytical queries on structured data, not for interactive, notebook-based exploration with Apache Spark on raw data lakes. While Redshift has a query editor, it''s not a Spark notebook environment.', 34, 'm_34_2', 3, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A large e-commerce company processes daily sales data, generating complex analytical reports that involve joining multiple large tables and performing aggregations. The data is highly structured and requires consistent, high-performance querying for business intelligence tools. Which AWS service is BEST suited for this workload, providing optimal performance for complex analytical queries?', '["Store data in Amazon S3 and use Amazon Athena for all complex analytical queries.","Use Amazon EMR Studio with Apache Hive for running complex SQL queries on S3.","Implement Amazon DynamoDB for storing the structured sales data and querying it directly.","Utilize Amazon Redshift, optimizing queries with distribution keys, sort keys, and Workload Management (WLM)."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{3}'::integer[], 'Option D (correct) is the best choice for high-performance, complex analytical queries on structured data. Amazon Redshift is a fully managed, petabyte-scale data warehouse service specifically designed for such workloads. Optimizing queries with distribution keys, sort keys, and Workload Management (WLM) ensures efficient data retrieval and processing for complex joins and aggregations.
Option A (incorrect) is suitable for ad-hoc queries on S3, but for consistent, high-performance complex analytical queries on structured data, Redshift typically offers superior performance and features like WLM for managing concurrent workloads.
Option B (incorrect) can run complex queries with Hive on EMR, but managing an EMR cluster for a dedicated data warehousing workload is generally more complex and less optimized for pure SQL analytics than Redshift. EMR Studio is more focused on interactive development.
Option C (incorrect) is a NoSQL database designed for high-performance, low-latency key-value and document data access, not for complex analytical queries involving large-scale joins and aggregations across multiple tables. It would be inefficient and costly for this use case.', 34, 'm_34_2', 4, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A research team regularly ingests new scientific experiment results, stored as JSON files in an Amazon S3 bucket. They need to perform ad-hoc SQL queries on this data for analysis and generate basic reports. The team wants to minimize infrastructure management and only pay for the queries they run. Which combination of services offers the LEAST infrastructure to manage?', '["Use Amazon Athena for querying the JSON data in Amazon S3, and Amazon QuickSight for reporting.","Set up an Amazon Redshift cluster to load and query the JSON data, then connect Amazon QuickSight.","Deploy an Amazon EMR cluster with Apache Hive to query the S3 data, and use Amazon QuickSight.","Develop a custom data processing application on Amazon EC2 to transform JSON, then use a self-managed BI tool."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) provides the least infrastructure to manage. Amazon Athena is a serverless query service that allows running SQL queries directly on data in Amazon S3 without provisioning or managing any servers. Amazon QuickSight is a serverless BI service that can connect directly to Athena, enabling report generation with minimal operational overhead.
Option B (incorrect) would require managing an Amazon Redshift cluster, which involves provisioning, scaling, and maintenance, thus increasing infrastructure management compared to a fully serverless approach.
Option C (incorrect) involves managing an Amazon EMR cluster, which, while powerful, requires more operational effort than Athena for simple ad-hoc querying and reporting.
Option D (incorrect) would involve significant infrastructure management by requiring EC2 instances for custom applications and a self-managed BI tool, which contradicts the goal of minimizing infrastructure management.', 34, 'm_34_2', 5, NULL, NULL);

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_34_1
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A financial services company receives transaction data continuously throughout the day, with new CSV files arriving every few minutes in an Amazon S3 bucket. This data needs to be immediately processed by an ETL job to transform and load it into a data warehouse. The company requires the LOWEST latency for processing new data. Which approach should be used?', '["Configure an AWS Glue job to run on a daily schedule, processing all new files from the previous day.","Set up Amazon S3 event notifications to trigger an AWS Glue job when new objects are created.","Implement a custom application on an Amazon EC2 instance that continuously polls the S3 bucket for new files.","Use AWS Batch to run a processing job every hour, checking for new files in the S3 bucket."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) provides the lowest latency for processing new data. Amazon S3 event notifications can be configured to trigger an AWS Glue job (or an AWS Lambda function, or send to SQS/SNS) immediately when new objects are created in the S3 bucket. This event-driven pattern ensures near real-time processing upon data arrival.
Option A (incorrect) would introduce significant latency, as data would only be processed once a day, failing to meet the requirement for immediate processing.
Option C (incorrect) is an anti-pattern (ap-3.1-001). Polling Amazon S3 for new files requires running infrastructure (EC2 instance) and introduces unnecessary latency due to the polling interval, consuming compute resources even when no new data is present.
Option D (incorrect) would also introduce latency, as data would only be processed hourly, not immediately upon arrival. AWS Batch is suitable for large-scale batch processing, but not for low-latency, event-driven scenarios.', 34, 'm_34_1', 6, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A media company needs to transform raw video metadata files, stored in Amazon S3, into a structured format for analysis. The transformation process involves complex data parsing and schema mapping. The company wants to minimize the operational overhead associated with managing compute infrastructure for this ETL workload. Which AWS service should they use?', '["Provision an Amazon EMR cluster and write custom Apache Spark jobs for ETL.","Utilize AWS Glue to create and run serverless ETL jobs.","Develop a custom Python script on an Amazon EC2 instance to perform the ETL process.","Use AWS Batch to schedule and run containerized ETL jobs on a cluster of EC2 instances."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the best choice for minimizing operational overhead for ETL workloads. AWS Glue is a fully managed, serverless ETL service that automatically provisions, configures, and scales the necessary compute resources. Users only need to define their ETL logic, significantly reducing the operational burden compared to managing clusters or instances.
Option A (incorrect) requires provisioning and managing an Amazon EMR cluster, which involves more operational overhead than AWS Glue, even though EMR is powerful for big data processing.
Option C (incorrect) would involve significant operational overhead, as it requires provisioning and managing Amazon EC2 instances, installing dependencies, and handling scaling and fault tolerance manually.
Option D (incorrect) while AWS Batch manages compute, it still requires defining and managing container images and job definitions, and it''s generally used for large-scale batch compute rather than fully serverless ETL like AWS Glue.', 34, 'm_34_1', 7, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A pharmaceutical company needs to run daily simulations that require significant compute resources, processing large datasets that can take several hours to complete. These simulations are not time-sensitive to the minute but must complete within a 24-hour window. The company wants the MOST cost-effective solution for these large-scale, non-time-sensitive batch processing workloads. Which AWS service should they choose?', '["Run the simulations as AWS Glue jobs, leveraging its serverless Spark environment.","Provision a dedicated Amazon EMR cluster that runs 24/7 to handle the daily simulations.","Use AWS Batch to dynamically provision and manage compute resources for the simulation jobs.","Deploy the simulation code on AWS Lambda functions, orchestrating them with AWS Step Functions."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the most cost-effective solution for large-scale, non-time-sensitive batch processing. AWS Batch dynamically provisions and manages compute resources (Amazon EC2 instances, including Spot Instances for cost savings) based on the workload, scaling up and down as needed. This ensures resources are only consumed when jobs are running, optimizing costs.
Option A (incorrect) while AWS Glue can handle large datasets, it''s primarily an ETL service and might not be the most cost-effective for general-purpose, long-running compute-intensive simulations compared to AWS Batch''s optimized resource management for batch jobs.
Option B (incorrect) would be very expensive as a dedicated Amazon EMR cluster running 24/7 would incur continuous costs, even when not actively processing, which is inefficient for daily, non-continuous workloads.
Option D (incorrect) AWS Lambda has execution duration limits (currently 15 minutes) and is designed for short-lived, event-driven functions, making it unsuitable for simulations that take several hours.', 34, 'm_34_1', 8, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A data engineering team needs to run a complex ETL job every night at 2:00 AM to aggregate data from various sources into a central data lake. The job involves reading from multiple Amazon S3 locations, performing transformations, and writing the results back to S3. The team requires a reliable and managed way to schedule and execute this daily ETL process. Which AWS service should they use?', '["Configure an AWS Glue job with a scheduled trigger.","Set up an Amazon S3 event notification to trigger an AWS Lambda function at 2:00 AM.","Implement a custom cron job on an Amazon EC2 instance to initiate the ETL process.","Use AWS Batch to run the ETL job, triggered by a custom application polling for the time."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the most appropriate and managed solution for a daily scheduled ETL job. AWS Glue jobs can be easily configured with scheduled triggers (using AWS Glue triggers, which leverage AWS EventBridge under the hood) to run at specific times, providing a reliable and serverless way to automate recurring ETL tasks.
Option B (incorrect) an Amazon S3 event notification is designed for event-driven processing upon data arrival, not for time-based scheduling. While Lambda can be scheduled with EventBridge, AWS Glue is specifically designed for ETL and is a more suitable choice for complex transformations.
Option C (incorrect) would introduce significant operational overhead. Managing an EC2 instance, maintaining the cron job, and ensuring its reliability and scaling falls outside the scope of a managed service approach.
Option D (incorrect) while AWS Batch can run scheduled jobs, AWS Glue is specifically designed for ETL, offering features like schema inference, data catalog integration, and job bookmarks that are highly beneficial for this scenario. Using a custom application to poll for time is also an anti-pattern for scheduling.', 34, 'm_34_1', 9, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A company receives customer feedback data as new files uploaded to an Amazon S3 bucket. This data needs to be processed automatically to extract keywords and sentiment as soon as it arrives. The processing should be event-driven to ensure minimal delay. Which architectural pattern BEST automates this processing upon new data arrival?', '["Configure a daily AWS Glue job to scan the S3 bucket for new files and process them.","Set up Amazon S3 event notifications to trigger an AWS Lambda function for processing.","Run a continuous Amazon EMR Studio notebook to monitor the S3 bucket for new files.","Implement a custom polling mechanism on an Amazon EC2 instance to detect new S3 objects."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) represents the best event-driven pattern for automating processing upon new data arrival. Amazon S3 event notifications can be configured to send a message to an Amazon SQS queue, publish to an Amazon SNS topic, or invoke an AWS Lambda function whenever new objects are created. This ensures immediate, serverless processing without polling.
Option A (incorrect) would introduce significant latency, as processing would only occur once a day, failing to meet the requirement for immediate, event-driven processing.
Option C (incorrect) is not suitable for continuous, automated, event-driven processing. EMR Studio notebooks are for interactive development and exploration, not for production-grade, low-latency automated pipelines.
Option D (incorrect) is an anti-pattern (ap-3.1-001). Polling Amazon S3 for new files requires running infrastructure (EC2 instance) and introduces unnecessary latency due to the polling interval, consuming compute resources even when no new data is present, and is less efficient than event notifications.', 34, 'm_34_1', 10, NULL, NULL);

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_34_4
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A healthcare provider ingests patient records into an Amazon S3 data lake. Before this data is used for analytics, it must undergo strict validation to ensure accuracy, completeness, and adherence to specific formats (e.g., date formats, valid codes). The data engineering team needs an automated way to define and evaluate these data quality rules. Which AWS service is BEST for automatically validating data quality?', '["Use Amazon CloudWatch to monitor the size of incoming files and alert on anomalies.","Implement custom AWS Lambda functions to perform data validation checks on each incoming file.","Utilize AWS Glue Data Quality to define and evaluate data quality rules within the data pipeline.","Perform manual data quality checks using Amazon Athena queries after data ingestion."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is specifically designed for automatically validating data quality within data pipelines. AWS Glue Data Quality allows users to define data quality rules (e.g., completeness, consistency, accuracy, validity) and evaluate them as part of AWS Glue ETL jobs. It provides a managed and integrated solution for data quality assessment.
Option A (incorrect) Amazon CloudWatch is for monitoring operational metrics and alarms, not for defining and evaluating data content quality rules. Monitoring file size doesn''t validate the data''s internal quality.
Option B (incorrect) while custom AWS Lambda functions can perform validation, this approach requires significant development and maintenance effort to build and manage the rule engine, making it less efficient than a managed service like AWS Glue Data Quality.
Option D (incorrect) performing manual data quality checks with Amazon Athena is reactive, time-consuming, and prone to human error. It does not provide the automated, proactive validation required before data consumption.', 34, 'm_34_4', 11, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A manufacturing company uses an existing AWS Glue ETL pipeline to process sensor data from factory equipment, transforming it before loading into an analytical database. They''ve identified issues with missing values and incorrect data types in the raw data, leading to unreliable reports. The team wants to integrate automated data quality checks directly into their existing Glue pipeline. Which approach BEST enforces data quality checks within this AWS Glue ETL pipeline?', '["Develop a separate Amazon EMR Studio notebook to manually inspect data quality after the Glue job runs.","Integrate AWS Glue Data Quality rules directly into the AWS Glue ETL jobs to validate data during processing.","Create Amazon CloudWatch alarms on Glue job failure rates, indicating potential data quality issues.","Write custom SQL validation queries in Amazon Athena to run against the transformed data post-load."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most effective way to enforce data quality within an existing AWS Glue ETL pipeline. AWS Glue Data Quality allows you to define rules and integrate them directly into your Glue jobs, enabling validation, anomaly detection, and quality scoring during the ETL process itself. This ensures data quality is checked proactively before data is consumed.
Option A (incorrect) involves manual inspection, which is not automated and would be reactive, not proactive, in catching data quality issues within the pipeline.
Option C (incorrect) CloudWatch alarms on job failure rates indicate operational issues, not specific data quality problems within the processed data. A job might succeed but still produce poor-quality data.
Option D (incorrect) running custom SQL validation queries in Amazon Athena after data is loaded is a reactive approach. Data quality issues would only be discovered post-processing, potentially after bad data has already been consumed or stored.', 34, 'm_34_4', 12, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A retail company collects customer order data from various online channels into an Amazon S3 data lake. Before this data is used for inventory management and sales forecasting, it''s crucial to identify and flag any data anomalies, such as duplicate orders, orders with invalid product IDs, or unusually high quantities. Which AWS service is BEST for identifying data anomalies and inconsistencies before consumption?', '["Use Amazon CloudWatch to set up alarms on the volume of data ingested into Amazon S3.","Manually review data samples using Amazon EMR Studio notebooks to spot inconsistencies.","Leverage AWS Glue Data Quality to define and evaluate rules for anomaly detection and data validation.","Create Amazon QuickSight dashboards to visualize raw data and visually identify outliers."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the best service for identifying data anomalies and inconsistencies before consumption. AWS Glue Data Quality allows you to define a wide range of rules to check for completeness, uniqueness, validity, and consistency. It can automatically evaluate these rules and provide data quality scores, helping to proactively identify and flag anomalous data within your data pipeline.
Option A (incorrect) Amazon CloudWatch alarms on data volume can detect ingestion issues but cannot identify specific data anomalies or inconsistencies within the data content itself.
Option B (incorrect) manual review, even with EMR Studio notebooks, is not scalable or automated for continuous anomaly detection in large, continuously arriving datasets. It''s suitable for ad-hoc exploration but not for production-grade quality assurance.
Option D (incorrect) while Amazon QuickSight can visualize data, relying solely on visual inspection to identify anomalies in raw data is inefficient, prone to human error, and not scalable for large datasets. It''s a reactive approach rather than a proactive, automated quality gate.', 34, 'm_34_4', 13, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A government agency needs to ensure that all data ingested into its data lake complies with strict regulatory standards. This involves defining specific rules for data types, ranges, and referential integrity across various datasets. The data engineering team requires a centralized and managed service to define, manage, and apply these data quality rules. Which approach is BEST to define and manage data quality rules for a data lake?', '["Store data quality rules as text files in Amazon S3 and implement custom validation logic in AWS Lambda.","Use AWS Glue Data Quality to define and manage data quality rules centrally.","Embed data quality checks directly into the SQL queries executed by Amazon Athena.","Develop custom scripts in Amazon EMR Studio notebooks to manually check data quality."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the best approach for defining and managing data quality rules centrally for a data lake. AWS Glue Data Quality provides a managed service where you can define, evaluate, and monitor data quality rules. It integrates directly with AWS Glue ETL jobs, allowing you to apply these rules consistently across your data processing pipelines and ensure compliance.
Option A (incorrect) storing rules in S3 and using custom Lambda functions requires significant development and maintenance effort to build a robust rule engine, manage rule versions, and integrate it into pipelines. This is less efficient and more complex than a managed service.
Option C (incorrect) embedding data quality checks directly into Amazon Athena SQL queries is reactive and not a centralized management approach. It would require duplicating logic across many queries and wouldn''t proactively prevent bad data from entering the lake.
Option D (incorrect) developing custom scripts in Amazon EMR Studio notebooks for manual checks is not an automated or centralized management solution. It''s suitable for ad-hoc analysis but not for enforcing continuous data quality standards.', 34, 'm_34_4', 14, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A logistics company processes shipment tracking data hourly. They need to continuously monitor the quality of this data, specifically looking for trends in missing tracking IDs or incorrect timestamps, and be alerted if data quality degrades. Which solution is BEST for monitoring data quality trends over time and alerting on degradation?', '["Set up Amazon CloudWatch alarms on the number of AWS Glue job failures.","Create Amazon QuickSight dashboards to visualize raw data and manually identify quality issues.","Utilize AWS Glue Data Quality to generate data quality scores and integrate with Amazon CloudWatch for alerting.","Implement custom scripts on Amazon EC2 to periodically sample data and report quality metrics."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the best solution. AWS Glue Data Quality allows you to define rules and evaluate them, generating data quality scores and metrics. These metrics can then be published to Amazon CloudWatch, where alarms can be configured to trigger notifications (e.g., via Amazon SNS) if data quality scores fall below a defined threshold or if specific rule violations increase. This provides automated, trend-based monitoring and alerting.
Option A (incorrect) CloudWatch alarms on Glue job failures indicate operational problems, not necessarily data quality degradation within successful job runs. A job might complete successfully but still produce low-quality data.
Option B (incorrect) while QuickSight can visualize data, relying on manual visual inspection for continuous data quality monitoring is not scalable, efficient, or proactive for detecting trends and automatically alerting on degradation.
Option D (incorrect) implementing custom scripts on Amazon EC2 for sampling and reporting would involve significant development and operational overhead to manage the EC2 instances, script execution, metric collection, and alerting mechanisms, which is less efficient than leveraging a managed service.', 34, 'm_34_4', 15, NULL, NULL);

-- ─────────────────────────────────────────
-- Module: m_34_3
-- ─────────────────────────────────────────

INSERT INTO public.mock_test_questions (mock_test_id, question_text, options, question_type, correct_answer, explanation, topic_id, module_id, question_order, pairs, matches)
VALUES
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A data engineering team uses AWS Glue ETL jobs to process daily incremental updates to a large customer database stored in Amazon S3. Each day, only new or modified records need to be processed to avoid reprocessing the entire dataset, which is time-consuming and costly. Which feature should be enabled to ensure the MOST efficient processing of only new data in recurring ETL jobs?', '["Configure the AWS Glue job to always perform a full scan of the Amazon S3 bucket for new data.","Enable AWS Glue job bookmarks for the ETL job.","Implement a custom S3 object versioning strategy to identify new files.","Use Amazon CloudWatch metrics to track the last processed file timestamp and filter data manually."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most efficient solution. AWS Glue job bookmarks track previously processed data, allowing AWS Glue ETL jobs to process only new or changed data since the last successful run. This significantly reduces processing time and costs for incremental loads, directly addressing the anti-pattern of not enabling Glue job bookmarks for incremental ETL processing (ap-3.3-001).
Option A (incorrect) performing a full scan every time is inefficient and costly, as it reprocesses the entire dataset, which is exactly what the company wants to avoid.
Option C (incorrect) while S3 object versioning tracks changes, it''s not designed for incremental ETL processing. Implementing a custom logic to leverage versioning for this purpose would be complex and less efficient than Glue job bookmarks.
Option D (incorrect) manually tracking the last processed timestamp and filtering data adds significant operational overhead and complexity. AWS Glue job bookmarks provide this functionality out-of-the-box in a managed way.', 34, 'm_34_3', 16, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A data pipeline relies on several AWS Glue ETL jobs that run hourly. The operations team needs to monitor the execution status, duration, and resource utilization (DPUs) of these jobs to ensure they are running efficiently and to troubleshoot any performance issues. Which AWS service is BEST for monitoring AWS Glue job execution status and resource utilization?', '["Utilize Amazon CloudWatch metrics and alarms for AWS Glue.","Analyze AWS CloudTrail logs for Glue API calls.","Use Amazon EMR Studio to view Glue job logs and metrics.","Create Amazon QuickSight dashboards based on Glue job history data."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{0}'::integer[], 'Option A (correct) is the best service for monitoring AWS Glue job execution status and resource utilization. AWS Glue automatically publishes detailed metrics (like DPU usage, job run status, duration, errors) to Amazon CloudWatch. CloudWatch allows you to visualize these metrics, set up alarms for specific thresholds (e.g., job failures, long durations), and get notifications, providing comprehensive operational monitoring.
Option B (incorrect) AWS CloudTrail logs API calls made to AWS Glue, which is useful for auditing and security, but it does not provide real-time performance metrics or detailed resource utilization for job runs.
Option C (incorrect) Amazon EMR Studio is for interactive data exploration and development, primarily with EMR clusters. It does not natively provide comprehensive monitoring for AWS Glue jobs.
Option D (incorrect) while Amazon QuickSight can be used to visualize historical job run data, it''s a BI tool and not designed for real-time operational monitoring and alerting on job execution status and resource utilization like CloudWatch.', 34, 'm_34_3', 17, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A critical data pipeline processes sensitive financial data using AWS Glue. If any of the Glue jobs fail, the operations team must be notified immediately to investigate and resolve the issue. They need a reliable and automated mechanism for receiving these alerts. Which solution is BEST for receiving immediate notifications when an AWS Glue job fails?', '["Periodically check the AWS Glue console manually for job status updates.","Configure an Amazon CloudWatch alarm on the AWS Glue job status metric, sending notifications to an Amazon SNS topic.","Implement a custom AWS Lambda function to poll the AWS Glue API for job status every minute.","Review AWS CloudTrail logs daily to identify any failed AWS Glue job API calls."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the best solution for immediate notifications. AWS Glue publishes job status metrics to Amazon CloudWatch. You can configure a CloudWatch alarm to trigger when a Glue job''s status changes to ''FAILED'' and send a notification to an Amazon SNS topic. SNS can then deliver these alerts via email, SMS, or integrate with other notification services, ensuring immediate and reliable communication.
Option A (incorrect) manually checking the AWS Glue console is not an automated or immediate solution and is highly inefficient for critical pipelines.
Option C (incorrect) while possible, implementing a custom AWS Lambda function to poll the Glue API introduces unnecessary operational overhead and complexity. CloudWatch alarms provide this functionality as a managed service, which is more robust and simpler to maintain.
Option D (incorrect) reviewing AWS CloudTrail logs daily is a reactive approach suitable for auditing, not for immediate operational alerting on job failures.', 34, 'm_34_3', 18, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A data engineering team observes that some of their AWS Glue ETL jobs are taking longer than expected to complete, leading to delays in downstream analytics. They need to identify the root cause of these performance bottlenecks, such as insufficient compute resources or inefficient data processing. Which AWS service is BEST for identifying performance bottlenecks in AWS Glue ETL jobs?', '["Analyze Amazon S3 access logs to understand data read/write patterns.","Review AWS CloudTrail logs for API calls made by the Glue jobs.","Examine Amazon CloudWatch metrics for AWS Glue job DPU utilization, duration, and error rates.","Use Amazon QuickSight to visualize historical job run success/failure rates."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{2}'::integer[], 'Option C (correct) is the best service for identifying performance bottlenecks in AWS Glue ETL jobs. AWS Glue automatically publishes detailed metrics to Amazon CloudWatch, including DPU (Data Processing Unit) utilization, job duration, shuffle bytes, and error rates. Analyzing these metrics allows engineers to pinpoint if a job is under-resourced, spending too much time on specific phases, or encountering errors that cause retries and delays.
Option A (incorrect) Amazon S3 access logs provide information about object access but do not directly reveal the internal performance characteristics or bottlenecks of an AWS Glue job.
Option B (incorrect) AWS CloudTrail logs API calls made by the Glue jobs, which is useful for auditing and security, but it does not provide the granular performance metrics needed to diagnose bottlenecks within the job''s execution.
Option D (incorrect) Amazon QuickSight can visualize historical job run success/failure rates, but it''s a BI tool and doesn''t provide the detailed, real-time operational metrics (like DPU utilization or shuffle activity) necessary to diagnose specific performance bottlenecks within a running or recently completed Glue job.', 34, 'm_34_3', 19, NULL, NULL),
  ('mock_32_all_1775006410483', 'A large social media company continuously ingests user activity data into an Amazon S3 data lake. This data arrives in small, frequent batches throughout the day. An AWS Glue ETL job runs daily to process this data, transforming it into an analytical format. To minimize compute costs and processing time, the company needs to ensure that the daily Glue job only processes the new data that arrived since the last successful run. Which approach is MOST cost-effective for processing large, continuously arriving datasets with AWS Glue?', '["Configure the AWS Glue job to reprocess the entire dataset from Amazon S3 every day.","Enable AWS Glue job bookmarks for the ETL job to track processed data.","Use AWS Batch to run a full data load job daily, processing all historical and new data.","Provision a large Amazon EMR cluster to handle the full dataset processing daily."]'::jsonb, 'mcq', '{1}'::integer[], 'Option B (correct) is the most cost-effective approach. AWS Glue job bookmarks track the state of previously processed data, allowing subsequent runs of an ETL job to process only new or changed data. This significantly reduces the amount of data scanned and processed, leading to lower compute costs and faster execution times. This directly addresses the anti-pattern of not enabling Glue job bookmarks for incremental ETL processing (ap-3.3-001).
Option A (incorrect) reprocessing the entire dataset daily is highly inefficient and costly, as it consumes unnecessary compute resources and time for data that has already been processed.
Option C (incorrect) using AWS Batch for a full data load daily would still incur costs for reprocessing the entire dataset, which is not cost-effective for incremental updates. While Batch can be cost-effective for large batch jobs, it doesn''t inherently provide incremental processing like Glue job bookmarks.
Option D (incorrect) provisioning a large Amazon EMR cluster for daily full dataset processing would be expensive due to the continuous compute resources required to reprocess all data, making it less cost-effective than incremental processing with Glue job bookmarks.', 34, 'm_34_3', 20, NULL, NULL);

